\section{Choose an analysis route}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Measurement:} \code{\link[=describe_mfrm_data]{describe_mfrm_data()}} reviews rating rows and the design;
\code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} estimates Person abilities, facet effects and category steps
within the selected model's scope. Read \code{summary(fit)$decision} before
interpreting the estimates. The example below starts with bundled ratings.
\item \strong{Assessment planning:} \code{\link[=mfrm_g_study]{mfrm_g_study()}} estimates observed-score variance
components; \code{\link[=mfrm_d_study]{mfrm_d_study()}} compares specified rater/task plans. Neither
requires an MFRM fit. G/Phi refer to the declared score and replication plan.
\item \strong{Rater feedback:} \code{\link[=interrater_agreement_table]{interrater_agreement_table()}} describes agreement,
\code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} reviews supported response-fit diagnostics, and
\code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}} / \code{\link[=analyze_dff]{analyze_dff()}} address specified interaction/group
questions. Severity, agreement and fit are different targets.
}
}

\section{Learning and reference}{

Use \code{vignette("mfrmr-workflow", package = "mfrmr")} for a first analysis
and importing your own ratings. The model-choice, G-theory, visual-diagnostics
and reporting tutorials address the corresponding decisions; list installed
tutorials with \code{vignette(package = "mfrmr")}.

\link{mfrmr_workflow_methods} connects the main result objects. Use
\link{mfrmr_methods_reference} to locate definitions and methodological sources,
or \code{\link[=mfrmr_output_guide]{mfrmr_output_guide()}} for the five central routes. Use
\code{mfrmr_output_guide("all")} to find specialist tables, figures or exports.
Function help gives the installed version's arguments, calculations and
limitations. Additional packages are required only by the features that name
them in their help. Cite \code{citation("mfrmr")} and the methods actually used.
}

\section{Saved analyses}{

Keep complete result objects with \code{\link[base:saveRDS]{base::saveRDS()}} and record the package
version and analysis settings. Reopening with \code{\link[base:readRDS]{base::readRDS()}} does not
update calculations. \link{mfrmr_compatibility_layer} distinguishes redisplaying,
recomputing derived results and refitting when updating older analyses.
For scoring against a portable fixed calibration, use
\link{mfrm_calibration_workflow}; this is a different artifact from a saved fit.
}

\section{Interpretation}{

Model, estimator, score coding, assignment and uncertainty assumptions must
match the intended use. A returned estimate, numerical convergence and an
available interval are separate results. The specialist help and returned
status/reason fields state the applicable restrictions; a review flag is
not an automatic judgment of rater quality or fairness.
}

\examples{
# Load the package
library(mfrmr)

# Load example ratings and look at the first six rows
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_operational")
head(ratings)

# Fit the model
fit <- fit_mfrm(
  data = ratings,
  person = "Person",
  facets = c("Rater", "Criterion"),
  score = "Score",
  rating_min = 1, rating_max = 4, category_policy = "preserve",
  method = "MML",
  model = "RSM"
)

# Read the interpretation status before examining or plotting estimates
results <- summary(fit)
results$decision
results$person_overview # One row summarizing person ability estimates
results$facet_overview  # One row per facet: number of levels, mean, SD, range

# Plot the results (Wright map) within the reported interpretation limits
plot(fit)
}
